/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>

  /* The layer. The mask keeps the base terrain underneath as grass. */
  create_terrain DESERT
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    terrain_mask 1
    land_percent 20
    number_of_clumps 8
  }

  /* THE MEASUREMENT. Asks for the whole map, matching a base AND a layer. */
  create_terrain SNOW
  {
    base_terrain GRASS
    base_layer DESERT
    land_percent 100
    number_of_clumps 400
  }

<OBJECTS_GENERATION>

  create_object FORAGE
  {
    number_of_objects 25
    set_gaia_object_only
  }

<CONNECTION_GENERATION>

/*
RMSTEST_49 - base terrain plus base layer: conjunction or disjunction?
Feeds: preview-design Sec.6.4, terrains.ts, Sec.15 item 24

QUESTION
Two guide lines disagree. The base-layer entry says a command applies only where
BOTH the base terrain and the layer match. An aside in the land-percent entry
says the appropriate base terrain OR base layer. Sec.6.4 follows the first, on
the reasoning that an attribute's own entry outranks an aside elsewhere, and
terrains.ts implements the conjunction.

The reasoning is sound and it is still reasoning. This project's own rule is to
prefer an observable, and this one is cheap.

It also re-tests a claim made by an earlier revision, which adopted the
disjunction to avoid the conjunction "under-filling every clump". If the
conjunction is correct, that under-fill is the engine's own behaviour and not a
defect to design around.

METHOD
Three layers of paint on one map.

  1. The whole map is grass.
  2. A desert LAYER over about a fifth of it. The mask makes it a layer rather
     than an ownership change, so the base terrain underneath stays grass
     everywhere - which is what makes the two models separable at all.
  3. A snow command asking for the whole map, matching base terrain grass AND
     base layer desert.

  conjunction -> snow appears ONLY where the desert layer is. Snow area is at
                 most the desert area, roughly a fifth of the map, and the two
                 patches coincide.
  disjunction -> snow covers essentially the whole map, because every tile
                 matches grass.

There is no middle outcome to worry about: the two predictions differ by a
factor of five in area and by everything in shape.

The clump count on the snow command is high on purpose. A low count could
under-fill for ordinary reasons and make a conjunction result look like a
disjunction that merely failed, which is exactly the confusion the earlier
revision's under-fill claim came out of.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), any player count. TWO RUNS.

READ IT WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches SNOW
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches DESERT
  Compare the two areas and the two bounding boxes. Coincident patches are the
  conjunction; snow covering the map is the disjunction.

Watch for one trap the earlier batches hit twice. This map is all land and
carries no water, no beach, no forest and no leaves, deliberately, because the
engine paints terrain of its own at a land and water boundary and around forest,
and an auto-generated band reads as a format error rather than as a result.
*/
